CALL BY REFERENCE
Interview answer:

In CALL BY REFERENCE, the address of the variable is passed to the Subprogram.
If the Subprogram modifies the variable, the changes are reflected in the Main Program.

Syntax in Main Program:
CALL "SUBPGM"
     USING BY REFERENCE WS-NUM.


Subprogram:
       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
       PROGRAM-ID. SUBPGM.

       DATA DIVISION.
       LINKAGE SECTION.

       01 LK-NUM PIC 9(3).

       PROCEDURE DIVISION USING LK-NUM.

           MOVE 500 TO LK-NUM.

           GOBACK.


Main Program:
       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
       PROGRAM-ID. MAINPGM.

       DATA DIVISION.
       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.

       01 WS-NUM PIC 9(3) VALUE 100.

       PROCEDURE DIVISION.

           CALL "SUBPGM"
                USING BY REFERENCE WS-NUM.

           DISPLAY "VALUE: " WS-NUM.

           STOP RUN.


Output:
VALUE: 500


Why 500?
Because the Subprogram modified the original variable through its reference.


CALL BY CONTENT
Interview answer:

In CALL BY CONTENT, a copy of the data is passed to the Subprogram.
Changes made inside the Subprogram will not affect the original variable in the Main Program.

Main Program:
       CALL "SUBPGM"
            USING BY CONTENT WS-NUM.


Subprogram:
       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
       PROGRAM-ID. SUBPGM.

       DATA DIVISION.
       LINKAGE SECTION.

       01 LK-NUM PIC 9(3).

       PROCEDURE DIVISION USING LK-NUM.

           MOVE 500 TO LK-NUM.

           GOBACK.


Original Main Program value:
WS-NUM = 100

After calling the Subprogram:
WS-NUM = 100
The original value remains unchanged.


